@flexiarg workshop/compensate-when-exclusion-is-unobtainable
@title Compensate When Exclusion Is Unobtainable
@keywords atomicity, exclusion, race, compensation, revert, detection, safety, liveness, tension
@audience consumer builders, witness authors, protocol designers
@tone corrective
@style pattern
@grade technique
@provenance The inbox-zero atomicity finding (2026-09-14): T2 requires atomic FEEL/commit; the runtime cannot provide it (editors do not share the consumer's lock; no cross-process mutual exclusion exists on the shared filesystem); codex-27 refused honestly (:atomic-feel-commit-unavailable) rather than shipping a timing window. Joe ruled compensating safety (2026-09-14): commit optimistically, detect races post-commit, revert and record. The TENSION is the pattern: safety demands exclusion; the world declines to provide it.
@review pending Joe's read (ruling on the approach given; the pattern text itself open).
@class process
@domain process
@family-note workshop/ family; the safety-valve twin of preserve-the-right-to-abstain — that pattern handles "cannot proceed"; this one handles "cannot exclude". Out of the math pool by construction.
@status workshop declaration 2026-09-14; NOT assayed; first instance pending (the inbox-zero compensating commit path).

! conclusion:
  When a safety property requires mutual exclusion the environment
  cannot provide, do not weaken the property silently and do not
  deadlock: restate the property in compensating form — act, detect
  violations immediately, undo, and record — then prove the restated
  property, not the original.

  + context:
    Atomic FEEL/commit was required to prevent committing under a live
    edit (U59's class). No lock spans human editors, agents, and the
    consumer on a shared filesystem. Refusing forever preserves safety
    but strands liveness: the work never flows.

  + IF:
    A rule demands exclusion ("never X while Y") and Y's actors cannot
    be made to hold the lock.

  + HOWEVER:
    Compensation has a detection window: a violation can occur and do
    brief harm before detection undoes it. The window must be BOUNDED
    (the detector's latency), STATED (as part of the claim), and priced
    (what transient harm costs inside the window). Compensation is not
    a license for unbounded optimism.

  + THEN:
    Restate the property in survivable form ("no X SURVIVES Y"): act
    after the best available check; re-verify immediately after the
    act; on detection, revert and emit a typed raced-finding; prove
    the restated property against the same negative controls that
    commissioned the original; and keep the honest refusal available
    for callers who need the stronger form.

  + BECAUSE:
    A property the environment cannot support is either abandoned
    silently (the facade), enforced into deadlock (the bureaucracy),
    or restated and re-proved (the honest engineering). The tension
    between safety and liveness is not resolved — it is PRICED: the
    detection window is the price, paid in the open.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Implement the compensating commit path with post-commit re-verify and revert.]
    next[Restate and prove T2's survivable form; the edit-after-snapshot negative control commissions the detector.]
    next[Record the detection-window bound in the certificate alongside the assumption fields.]
